sexual harassment in the workplace is a  topic today in our ongoing series we  call it issues it matter the subject is  you know is dominating headlines as  people share their personal stories of  harassment nearly 70 women have come  forward with accusations against Harvey  Weinstein that followed reporting by New  York Times correspondent and CBS News  contributor Jody Cantor and another  reporter Megan tui they exposed decades  of Weinstein's alleged harassment  The Times technology reporter Katie  Benner spent months digging into sexual  harassment claims of women entrepreneurs  in Silicon Valley and business reporter  Emily Steele and a colleague  investigated the nearly 50 million  dollars known to have been spent on  settling sexual harassment claims  against former Fox News star Bill  O'Reilly Jody Cantor Katie Benner and  Emily Steele join us now good morning  great to have all of you here and  congratulations on all of your  incredible reporting dirty let me start  with you because you broke the Weinstein  story about a month ago now it's  incredible to see essentially this  tsunami now of accusations that have  come as a result I think of sort of the  hashtag me to the sharing the idea that  you could share the story and then it  would be reported fairly and accurately  yes I think one sort of meaning of the  Weinstein story is that it is important  to come forward because we've now  established a 40 year timeline of  allegations so for people who knew about  his behavior early on in some of them  like Quentin Tarantino have come forward  publicly and said I should have said  something much earlier and so part of  what the Weinstein demonstrates is that  even though coming forward can be very  very hard for a woman there can  potentially be a cost to other people of  not doing it okay you gave I thought a  very graphic descriptive analogy about  sexual harassment in Silicon Valley you  said this it's like a healthy-looking  log it's like turning over a  healthy-looking log to find decay and  bugs underneath what made the women you  spoke to decide I'm speaking out and I'm  speaking out now well part of it was the  reporting that Emily and Michael Schmidt  had done around Fox so their story came  on April and after that happened in the  newsroom we saw we saw the story and we  thought this is a bigger issue than just  Fox we really need to dive into it we  had a lot of meetings about  as I reached out to women in Silicon  Valley I would send them Emily's story  and they would read it and they would  see how seriously we took the issue and  some of them came to me and said that  they'd read it on their own and that  made them and inspire them to speak out  so I think part of it was what Jodi said  this sort of chorus of voices gathering  over the course of the year is it was  really really powerful I mean Emily you  first reported sorry you first reported  those allegations against Bill O'Reilly  and at Fox News  how much has changed since then I think  a lot has changed I think when we were  doing our reporting it was kind of at  this time last year we are still digging  up information still trying to get  people to talk to us people were very  hesitant to talk about this issue and it  was really hard we had one woman who  went on the record with her story it was  very hard to convince her to do that I  flew out to LA and did a Pilates class  with her to build a relationship to try  to get her to talk and to tell her that  so many other women have been silenced  and how important it was that she had of  waste she really needed to talk and what  we've seen now with Katie's reporting  and Jodi and Megan's reporting is that  people are talking more about this issue  women are finding their voices but also  the world is listening they don't know  they'll be believed in and how a  corporation is changing how our  institution is changing you know and  what does it mean it's such a historic  change what is the emerging definition  of sexual harassment well that's one  thing that we've talked a lot about is  that the actual legal definition of  sexual harassment is quite gray and that  there are these standards that the  behavior needs to be severe and  pervasive and have a negative impact on  your career in order to bring a legal  case and that's one of the issues that's  really complicated this whole how do you  think people are confused because I  think there are times somebody can say  something lewd and inappropriate but it  doesn't mean that you were being  harassed in a sexual manner that's just  somebody who's just a jerk and you want  nothing to do with them do you think  that we have to have very specific  things about what sexual harassment  means in the workplace well I think what  we see now and we saw this working  together as a team and kind of  developing our reporting together in  private is that there are a lot of  strong patterns to what's happening that  are coming in to you now  in all of our stories there were women  who were pretty junior in their careers  who were allegedly harassed by more  senior men and the problem is that young  women's careers can be cut off and this  can have an impact on who rises and  succeeds in the workplace we saw very  common means of intimidation Emily and  Megan and I reported a lot on  settlements and you wrote reported a lot  on non-disclosure agreements and so what  working together it was more than just  looking at fox or at Weinstein right  Silicon Valley what we were trying to  get at was the bigger picture of why is  the system broken and what are the  common patterns across all of these  different industries our NDA's guys part  of the problem because I don't think any  woman who signs the NDA thinks that she  could be jeopardizing or hurting another  woman should they make a non-disclosure  it's not just that it's part of the  problem because it jeopardizes someone  else also jeopardizes yourself I spoke  with lots of people who sign NDA's who  regretted it almost immediately they  really felt like they had been treated  unfairly and oftentimes they're  undervalued I talked to a woman who  signed an NDA and the amount of money  she got was a pins they they compensated  her for her therapy bills to deal with  what had happened to her and when she  really stepped back after it happened  right after the incident she felt very  very sad and depressed she was not in a  good negotiating position and then in a  few months later she realized she'd  really only been given I think $10,000  for a lifetime of sign these agreements  have a legal standing so there's debate  over that now their husbands there have  been some attorneys have come out and  said that you can break them and the  consequences are not as dire as your  workplace but have you believed I've  heard you today Katie this is more than  just a he-said she-said story you see it  economically as well oh absolutely like  if you look at where the consequences  have been so with Weinstein the  consequences were severe and Swift and  with Emily's story the consequences were  very severe for Bill O'Reilly because  there was a lot of economics at stake  there the advertisers pulled out so they  couldn't support those people in Silicon  Valley a lot of these investors owned  their own businesses so where is the  economic impact there one of the people  I covered he had already shot down his  investment firm so there's been no  negative impact for him I don't I think  some of the reporting that all of you  have done though it's not only exposed  not just the courage for women to speak  out that you have institutional  static lawyers and everybody in place to  stop this from going anywhere with Fox  and with Bill O'Reilly he said no one  ever had a complaint against him he had  it written in his contract that he  couldn't be fired over an allegation  that wasn't proven in court right and  what we know is when Fox renewed  O'Reilly's contract in February of this  year the company knew have six  allegations against him and we now know  that those settlements those settlements  for those six allegations have totaled  forty five million dollars and Jody in  Harvey Weinstein's contract that was  negotiated with the Weinstein Company  that also had some protections for him  absolutely so you begin to ask who  negotiated that contract and what  incidents did they know about and what  were they protecting against and what  were they trying to bury the kind of  moral gravity of our story shifted when  we found in the 2015 memo written by  Lauren O'Connor the detailed sexual  harassment in the company it was pages  and pages long a lot of detailed  descriptions and here this brave woman  had come forward within the company and  her complaint had essentially been  buried these are simply two changes  that's the that's what everybody wants  to know we have this conversation what  now is going to change well the hope  first of all isn't it more women will  feel comfortable coming forward and that  the shame and stigma which you know part  of our teamwork over the years was  really about getting women comfortable  with coming forward and making them feel  like they were part of something larger  and strategizing about how we could  treat our sources as sensitively as  possible but also give them strength and  confidence in telling their stories so  hopefully that is something that is a  little easier now but I think there are  also big policy questions on the table  right I mean should NDA's be used to  cover up abuse or harassment should  companies be required to report  settlements so that there's some sort of  public trail about what happened all  right he's ready Cantor am I still Katie  Benner thank you all we really  appreciate it yes
 

